.sisyphus/

# OpenCode agent state
.omo/

# Kilo project config. `.git/info/exclude` already lists `.kilo/`, but that file is
# per-clone and untracked, so a decision recorded in a plan lived only on the machine
# that wrote it. The two negations exist because git cannot re-include a file whose
# parent directory is excluded: `.kilo/plans/` re-includes the directory, and
# `.kilo/plans/*.md` re-includes the documents inside it. Everything else under
# `.kilo/` — personas, skills, commands, session state, and worktrees — stays out.
#
# Verified on git 2.55.0, and the two behave differently: the negations DO un-ignore
# `.kilo/` and `.kilo/plans/` themselves, but a `.md` file *inside* them is still
# reported ignored by `git check-ignore`, attributed to `.git/info/exclude:9`. The
# documented precedence puts `.gitignore` above `$GIT_DIR/info/exclude`; for a path
# under an excluded directory the exclude file wins instead. So on a clone whose
# `info/exclude` carries the Kilo block — every clone Kilo itself has set up — a new
# plan is invisible to `git status` and to `git add -A`, and has to be added with
# `git add -f`. Once tracked it stays tracked; ignore rules only govern untracked
# files. A fresh clone (and CI) has the stock `info/exclude` and needs no `-f`.
.kilo/*
!.kilo/plans/
!.kilo/plans/*.md

# Coverage and test output
coverage/
test-results/
playwright-report/
blob-report/
.artifacts/
var/

# Vitest cache
.vite/
